      "stdout": "Done.\n\nI found the requested files mounted in this environment at /workspace/in/db rather than the original <HOME>/... path, so I edited the live workspace copies there.\n\nFiles updated/created:\n- /workspace/in/db/migration.sql\n- /workspace/in/db/preflight_report.md\n- /workspace/in/db/rollback.sql\n- /workspace/in/db/postcheck.sql\n- /workspace/in/db/migration_report.md\n\nWhat changed\n- migration.sql now:\n  - runs inside an explicit BEGIN IMMEDIATE transaction\n  - rebuilds users safely instead of dropping data\n  - preserves all user ids and all orders.user_id references\n  - preserves created_at values\n  - adds status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'active'\n  - enforces email TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE plus CHECK(length(trim(email)) > 0)\n  - deterministically cleans dirty users exactly as required:\n    - u4 -> ada+u4@example.com\n    - u5 -> missing+u5@example.invalid\n    - u6 -> missing+u6@example.invalid\n  - is safe to run twice on the migrated dataset without duplicating/corrupting rows\n\n- preflight_report.md documents:\n  - duplicate/null/blank email issues\n  - dependent orders for u4, u5, u6\n\n- rollback.sql:\n  - restores the old users schema shape: id, email, name, created_at\n  - preserves user row count and order row count\n  - keeps order references on the same user ids\n\n- postcheck.sql verifies:\n  - user/order row counts\n  - dependent orders for u4/u5/u6\n  - cleaned dirty-user emails\n  - preserved created_at values\n  - schema/index constraints for email/status\n  - no null/blank/duplicate emails and no null statuses\n\n- migration_report.md summarizes:\n  - migration strategy\n  - dirty-data cleanup\n  - idempotency approach\n  - rollback behavior/limitation\n  - postcheck queries to run\n\nValidation performed\nI validated the scripts with Python’s sqlite3 module because the sqlite3 CLI is not installed here.\n\nVerified results:\n- schema.sql loads successfully\n- migration.sql runs successfully\n- running migration.sql a second time preserves the same 6 users and 4 orders\n- cleaned emails are exactly:\n  - u4 = ada+u4@example.com\n  - u5 = missing+u5@example.invalid\n  - u6 = missing+u6@example.invalid\n- all statuses are active\n- created_at values are preserved\n- rollback.sql restores the users table shape to:\n  - id, email, name, created_at\n- orders remain intact after rollback\n\nOne important rollback note\n- rollback restores the old schema shape, but it keeps the cleaned email values rather than reintroducing the original dirty values. This is documented in migration_report.md.\n",
